{
  "image_name": "01c91a46f5e85e5772902f4b9c8978b2.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how immune-cell activation is linked to metabolic reprogramming and, in turn, to different immune functions. It starts with naïve T cells, resting B cells, and macrophages receiving stimulation through antigen receptors and innate receptors, along with co-stimulatory signals. These inputs drive a central shift in cell metabolism.\n\nFrom that common metabolic switch, the diagram separates into two broad outcomes. One branch leads to inflammatory cell states, including M1 macrophages, effector T cells, and activated B cells. These cells are associated with an anabolic metabolic program marked by increased glycolysis and glutaminolysis and reduced oxidation, supporting rapid activation and inflammatory responses.\n\nThe other branch leads to regulatory or long-lived states, including M2 macrophages, Treg cells, memory T cells, and memory B cells. These are linked to a more catabolic metabolism, especially fatty acid oxidation and pyruvate oxidation, which appears to support immune surveillance, persistence, and suppression rather than acute inflammation.\n\nThe scheme also highlights signaling regulators that bias these fates: HIFs, AKT, and mTOR on the inflammatory/anabolic side, and FOXP3 and PGC1α on the regulatory/catabolic side. PD-1 and CTLA4 are shown as inhibitory influences on the inflammatory pathway.",
